%I A400470 #37 Oct 15 2026 17:30:37
%S A400470 19,15,15,14,14,21,14,13,13,13,12,10,8,8,7,5
%N A400470 The size of the smallest boundary square in the simple perfect squared squares of order n.
%C A400470 5 is the smallest size possible in a simple perfect squared square (or rectangle), for simple perfect squared squares this bound is not reached until order 36. Ian Gambini proved 5 is the lower bound in his thesis and found examples in order 44 and 58. The order 36 lower bound of 5 was found in 2026, in a squared square generated by Jim Williams in 2020. Jim Williams enumerated SPSS orders 33, 34, 35 and 36, the terms a(33)-a(36) were extracted from his work.
%H A400470 Stuart E. Anderson, <a href="https://www.squaring.net/sq/sr/spsr/spsr_boundary.html">Simple Perfects by Boundary Rules and Conditions</a>.
%H A400470 Stuart E. Anderson, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.0599">Compound Perfect Squared Squares of the Order Twenties</a>, arXiv:1303.0599 [math.CO], 2013.
%H A400470 A. J. W. Duijvestijn, <a href="https://www.squaring.net/sq/ss/spss/o21/spsso21.pdf">Illustration for a(21)=19</a>.
%H A400470 Ian Gambini, <a href="http://alain.colmerauer.free.fr/alcol/ArchivesPublications/Gambini/carres.pdf">Quant aux carrés carrelés</a>, Thesis, Université de la Méditerranée Aix-Marseille II, 1999, section 3.6.4.
%e A400470 a(21) = 19, see the link to Duijvestijn's square.
%Y A400470 Cf. A006983, A195984, A400444, A002839.
%K A400470 nonn,more
%O A400470 21,1
%A A400470 _Stuart E Anderson_, Sep 27 2026
